// @check
// @error-at 17:5 an `if` body requires braces: `if cond { … }`
// @error-at 18:15 cannot assign string to 'n' of type i32
// THE PAYOFF of the lossless-recovery flag (DECISIONS.md, 2026-09-01): a file
// whose EVERY parse diagnostic came from a provably lossless recovery is
// TYPECHECKED anyway, so the missing brace and the real type error two lines
// down are reported together. Until this landed, `checkSrc` returned at
// `P.diags.length > 0` and the type error was invisible until the brace was
// typed — which, in an editor compiling per keystroke, meant a file lost every
// squiggle it had the moment a body was half-written.
//
// The recovery is lossless because the arm IS the statement: `parseBracedBody`
// hands `print(1)` to `parseStmt` exactly as a braced body would have, dropping
// no token and inventing none, so the type error below is read off the program
// the user actually wrote. `parser/lossy-parse-suppresses-typecheck.vl` is the
// other side of the rule.
const c = true
if c print(1)
const n: i32 = "hi"
print(n)
